<p><a href="https://www.philosophy.ox.ac.uk/people/gabriele-a-taylor" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gabriele Taylor</a>, emeritus fellow in philosophy at St. Anne&#8217;s College, University of Oxford, has died.<span id="more-58178"></span></p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-58179 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/taylor-gabriele.jpg" alt="" width="200" height="240" /></p>
<p>Gabriele Taylor was known for her work in moral philosophy, especially moral psychology. She is the author of <em><a href="https://global.oup.com/academic/product/pride-shame-and-guilt-9780198246206?cc=us&amp;lang=en&amp;" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pride, Shame, and Guilt: Emotions of Self-Assessment</a> </em>(1985) and <em><a href="https://global.oup.com/academic/product/deadly-vices-9780199548682?cc=us&amp;lang=en&amp;" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Deadly Vices</a> </em>(2006), among other works, which you can learn more about <a href="https://philpapers.org/s/Gabriele%20Taylor" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.</p>
<p>She earned her B.Phil at St. Anne&#8217;s and taught there for a while, and then moved to Australia to teach at University College of Canberra. She returned to St. Anne&#8217;s in 1964 and was a fellow there until her retirement in 1996.</p>
<p>Her collected essays, as well as a memoir, will be published next year, edited by her former student, <a href="https://www.constantinesandis.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Constantine Sandis</a>.</p>
<p>You can listen to an interview with Gabriele Taylor about her life and work, conducted in 2011, <a href="https://podtail.com/en/podcast/st-anne-s-college/an-interview-with-gabriele-taylor-by-dr-nigel-bowl/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.</p>
<p>St. Anne&#8217;s College has posted a brief memorial notice <a href="https://www.st-annes.ox.ac.uk/in-memoriam-gabriele-taylor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.</p>
